    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Can you provide a summary of the competing legal philosophies regarding the Second Amendment, specifically focusing on the tension between originalism and the living constitutionalist approach?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The debate over the Second Amendment centers on whether the right to keep and bear arms is limited to militia service (originalism) or is an individual right against the government (living constitutionalism). Originalists, like Justice Scalia in *District of Columbia v. Heller*, argue that the text and Framing intent confirm a pre-existing right for defendable citizens unrelated to a specific army. Conversely, living constitutionalists often look to the \"well-regulated militia\" clause in the Preamble to the Amendment to argue it implies a collective right inextricably linked to state control and public safety. This fundamental disagreement on historical meaning and the purpose of the text creates the most significant political and legal friction in modern American jurisprudence.",
      "source": "target",
